# ARBIVERSE – Individual Contribution

## My main responsibility

I coded Arbiverse, a single playable web game with three phases: Phase I Tulip Mania, Phase II Thai Baht Crisis (triangular FX arbitrage) and Phase III JPY Carry Trade. My main responsibility was to turn the group’s financial logic and scoring files into working game rules, calculations and scoring.

I built each phase from scratch: the rounds and timers, buy and sell offers, cash and inventory, FX arbitrage across onshore and offshore markets, yen borrowing and repayment, fees, profit and loss, and the final settlement. I coded the scoring for all three phases out of 100 exactly as the scoring files describe, and wrote the debrief screens that explain each score and end with an economic lesson. I also added the sound effects, mini-charts, warning pop-ups and the menu that connects the three phases into one game.

I tested every phase from start to final debrief, reran the same tests after each fix, and corrected the problems that appeared, such as the Phase II amount field, the Max button and scores that did not add up. I also supported my teammates with layout design, game testing, financial logic and rule definition, and applied Uyên’s design and test feedback in the code.

